I'd like to know what local patches people have running on their systems.
I have one patch on my own game that the dev team will try to fit in 3.1,
and I have some other ideas that will probably never make it into official
nethack, because I don't think everybody else would like to play this way.

For instance, the idea which I sent to the dev team was rustproofing: if an
armor or weapon is made of metal, rustproof, and known to be rustproof, it
is identified as rustproof in the inventory ("a blessed rustproof +5
longsword named Excalibur").  Items are known to be rustproof if they
really are and either they have been identified or the characteristic
message ("%s is not affected") has been displayed with respect to that
item.  The shimmering gold field does not identify the rustproof status of
the item.  Rustproof items may be wished for by asking for them the way
they appear in the inventory.

Another idea I've had is causing dwarvish mattocks not to appear as "huge
shiny swords", but I finally suspended my disbelief.  Now I pretend that
they are under a dwarvish enchantment to *appear* as swords until by
magical means I reveal their true nature.  It used to bother me because a
mattock is a digging implement that looks more like a pickaxe than a
sword.  I couldn't figure out why nethack uses such a description for it.
I asked the net why, and mostly what I got back was that TSR started it,
and nethack just continues the tradition.  I decided for myself that TSR
probably started it because in The Hobbit, in the War of Five Kings, the
dwarvish army came equipped with "two handed mattocks".  Somebody probably
read that, didn't bother to check the dictionary for a word they didn't
recognize, and instituted the idea that a mattock was a sword.  Although,
once you think about it, given that dwarves seem to have an intrinsic
talent for ripping through earth, and that metal armor is essentially
earth, and that dwarves (according to The Hobbit) are generally strong for
their size and these guys were strong even for dwarves, the idea of facing
one of these dwarves wielding such a weapon is frightening.  They would
have been *less* intimidating wielding huge shiny swords.  Oh, well...

Another idea that I intend to implement after I finish my first win with a
wizard is to redo the use of spells.  The idea that you tend to forget
spells you have memorized if you use them often is counterintuitive to me.
I will want instead to calculate the power required to cast a spell, and if
it exceeds your current power, take your power level to zero, take the
difference out of your hit points, send a message along the lines of "You
can't control the spell--More--You are jolted by psychic backlash!", and
either fail to cast the spell, or cast it destructively by having the magic
missiles hit your pet instead of the monster or something like that.  And
I would triple or quadruple the power required to cast a spell to make it
a real constraint (as it is, I completely ignore the power stat, and I
play a wizard!).

Yet another idea I intend to implement eventually is to change the way Elves
start up.  I tried one the other day, and was really surprised at his start
up stats (His charisma was 7).  I started up several games, immediately
quitting each time, to see if it was an aberration.  My elves always
started with low charisma!  My image of elves is such that they would start
off with low strength-- maybe a point or two lower than a wizard's, to
account for their size, high dexterity-- in the range you would expect for
a wizard's intelligence, variable intelligence and wisdom, and high
charisma-- in the same range as their dexterity.  The current elf intrinsic
usage feels "right" to me, so I wouldn't want to change that.

What other ideas are out there?

